opinions about hypersonic weapons aren't  peaceful some people consider them game  changers because given their extreme  speed it would be impossible to defend  against their attack others say they are  but the latest in a long line of  overhyped weapon systems and are  relatively easy to defend against  because they fly more slowly as they  near their target than ballistic  missiles with comparable ranges others  claim that they will strengthen  conventional deterrence still others  argue they will create instability  between nuclear powers by fueling a  dangerous arms race this debate has  gained greater attention in recent years  as the united states russia and china  have made their own progress in the  field but before explaining what the  three powers have in store we need to  explain what a hypersonic missile is and  differentiate it from a ballistic  missile we are helped by various  entities including the russian council  for international affairs but also the  american missile defense advocacy  alliance that in an article states that  there are two requirements to label a  weapon as hypersonic a speed greater  than mach 5 so more than 5 times that of  sound and the ability to change course  and perform maneuvers both vertical and  horizontal while remaining within the  atmosphere this differentiates it from  ballistic missiles that instead travel  outside the atmosphere and although they  can reach hypersonic speeds and very  often do they have fixed arc  trajectories and poor maneuverability  this image is worth a thousand words an  example of this is the minuteman iii the  us intercontinental ballistic missile  which can reach mach 23 on descent and  could strike russia from silos located  in the western united states within  about 30 minutes from launch traditional  cruise missiles on the other hand fly at  subsonic speeds less than mach 1 and  supersonic speeds between mach 1 and  mach 5. the hypersonic missiles then  divides into two species hypersonic  cruise missiles and hypersonic glide  vehicles the former are powered by  high-speed engines called scramjets and  thanks to them they keep a constant  hypersonic speed and altitude during the  entire flight in contrast the latter  since they have no propulsion system are  usually launched on top of a booster  rocket and then glide through the  atmosphere toward their target at  hypersonic speeds both types can carry  conventional or nuclear payloads the  advantage of these missiles over their  ballistic rivals is quite obvious as  reported by the expert cameron tracy  since hypersonic weapons fly through the  atmosphere they can take advantage of  aerodynamic forces to course correct  over most of their flight path making it  difficult for enemy radars to predict  where they will end ballistic missiles  in contrast can execute corrective  maneuvers only for brief periods during  their initial ascent out of the  atmosphere and final descent to earth  however glide vehicles while having this  bonus suffer from a lower average speed  than ballistic missiles due to  atmospheric flight and above all suffer  from extreme overheating in the  subsystems which not only affects the  aerodynamics of the missile but also  blocks communication with satellites or  other guidance sources thereby worsening  control something that apparently  doesn't affect hypersonic cruise  missiles since they fly slower than  rocket launch systems beyond the debate  surrounding these weapons one thing is  for sure they pose a challenge to many  existing types of medium and long-range  air missile defenses u.s defense  officials say this will require the  development of wholly new intercept  systems supporting technologies and the  new sensor architecture the ones of most  concern are glide vehicles so much so  that the us is developing defense  systems focused on eliminating this type  of threat in the end phase of its flight  since as expert james acton claims it  will travel slower just before reaching  its target due to resistance with the  air thereby being able to be intercepted  more easily who is funding the  development of these engineering marvels  let's take a trip to the united states  i'll tell you just a few numbers the  trump administration had allocated a  total of nearly 6 billion dollars for  all hypersonic related research and  development for fiscal years 2020 and  2021. in may 2021 the biden  administration in its budget request for  fiscal year 2022 added 3.8 billion  dollars with the intention of  accelerating trump's plans by  strengthening conventional deterrence  against russia and china i say  conventional because washington  currently has no plans to keep  hypersonic systems with nuclear warheads  unlike moscow and beijing some pentagon  officials say that the rush to  hypersonic programs is due to the fact  that current american missile  capabilities such as the sea launched  the tomahawk cruise missile or the joint  air to surface standoff missile lack the  maneuverability and speed necessary to  counter evolving adversary threats  especially those from china others  however leverage the competitive aspect  meaning that as michael griffin reports  the united states needs to develop such  weapons to allow it to match what its  adversaries are doing then there is a  debate in the expert community about how  many weapons the us should deploy some  speak of thousands to deter chinese  hypersonic attacks against carriers and  airfields and other critical targets in  the western pacific others speak of  dozens because they should be used  sparingly and exploited for their  surgical strike capabilities in any case  given the internal differences of  opinion within the pentagon hypersonic  weapons will put some pretty significant  future funding challenges on the table  instead what treasures does china hide  china has always been reluctant to  publicly reveal information about its  nuclear forces and likewise very little  is known about beijing's development of  hypersonic weapons overall china's  motivations appear to be to prevail over  american missile defenses and outclass  its offensive weapons in the event of a  war in asia according to expert tongzao  almost all chinese experts agree that  the ability to penetrate the defensive  systems is the most important feature of  hypersonic weapons there are at least  five programs underway in china  including the dongfeng 17 a medium-range  ballistic missile that us officials  estimate has a range of 18 to  2500 kilometers and the speed between  mach 5 and mach 10. we don't know  whether conventional or nuclear but u.s  intelligence has rated it as dual  capable then there would be a  nuclear-capable hypersonic glide missile  prototype known as the shinkong 2 or  steri sky 2 that would be successfully  tested in 2018. instead russia has  publicly unveiled the development of  hypersonic weapons saying they are  intended to ensure that moscow can evade  any future us anti-missile defenses  putin himself said in september 2020  that the u.s withdrawal from the  anti-ballistic missile treaty in 2002  forced russia to develop hypersonic  weapons and in march 2018 when he  introduced a number of new nuclear  weapon launch systems he also talked  about some hypersonic weapons such as  the avant-garde a hypersonic glide  missile that was mounted on ss19  intercontinental missile successfully  tested and deployed in 2019. among those  intended for use in the regional context  i'd mentioned that sircon a c-launched  hypersonic cruise missile and the kinzel  a hypersonic ballistic missile launched  from the only modified fighter bomber  capable of doing so the mig-31k  and deployed a few days ago in the  ukrainian village of deliatin to hit an  underground missile and ammunition depot  i open a parenthesis on this there would  be something strange in moscow's  narrative the images released by the  kremlin are overdrawn and it's difficult  to think that it could have operated  freely in ukrainian airspace so far west  of the front line where there is no  russian air superiority and since  ukrainian air defenses are still  standing they could have detected it and  shut it down some journalists then  reported that the inhabitants of the  surrounding areas wouldn't have heard  any big explosion make your own  judgments but now we come to the  conclusion given the potential risks to  stability posed by hypersonic weapons  arms control is a valuable tool for  mitigating them but one that may remain  utopian in recent years experts have  begun to explore several possible  options the first to measure to ensure  strategic stability is the strengthening  of mutual trust between powers which can  be translated both in the collaboration  to avoid dangerous consequences even in  the absence of formal treatise and in  the increase of transparency thereby  sharing technical details and  information another tool is the banning  of certain types of weapons such as  long-range ballistic missiles see the  russian kinsel and the american agm-183  and where this isn't possible minimizing  the risks posed by these systems by  putting on paper bilateral or trilateral  agreements limiting their number we  don't know what the future holds but  it's likely that none of these  cooperative arms control concepts will  gain support let alone become the basis  for formal agreements between powers  well we are done for today i thank you  all for your attention and see you in  the next video ciao
 

